From: theycallmeboxy <82409831+theycallmeboxy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:52:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] panfrost/cs: O(1) pending-load check and an inline fast path
 for CS allocation

Every CS instruction goes through cs_alloc_ins_block() and, once per
register operand, cs_flush_load_to(). In a CPU profile of Batman: Arkham
Asylum's DXVK submission thread they were 5.05% and 3.14% of PanVK's
samples (cs_move32_to, which is little more than both, another 3.73%),
and neither was inlined.

cs_flush_load_to() walked up to 16 registers testing each bit of the
256-bit pending-load bitset, which is nearly always empty while recording
draws. The registers of an operand lie in at most two consecutive words of
the bitset, so shift those two words into a 64-bit value and test it
against the mask in one AND.

cs_alloc_ins_block() called cs_reserve_instrs() for every instruction,
which re-checks validity, the allocation mode, the lazy root chunk and the
chunk capacity. Take the common case inline: no open block, a valid
growable CS with a live chunk that still has room for the instructions
plus the chunk-linking sequence. That is exactly the case in which
cs_reserve_instrs() returns true without doing anything; everything else
goes to the unchanged out-of-line path.

The emitted command streams are bit-identical: a host-side differential
test builds the old and the new header into one binary and replays 20000
random instruction sequences through both (moves, 1-16 register loads and
stores at every base register, waits, if/else, loops with break/continue,
match, maybe, LOAD_IP, chunk overflow with 64-2000 instruction chunks,
fixed buffers, allocation failure, dirty tracking): 68M instructions, all
bytes, sizes and dirty sets equal. A one-line mutation of the bitset
extraction is caught on the first sequence. Host timing of a 14-instruction
draw-like sequence (Apple M-series, -O2): 49-55 ns -> 26 ns.

